.TH LOREGD 8 "September 2026" "Peios" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
loregd \- serve local Peios registry hives
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B loregd
.I HiveName=DatabasePath
.RI [ HiveName=DatabasePath
\&...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B loregd
is the default implementation of the Peios
.B registryd
role. It persists each configured registry hive in an SQLite database and
serves it to the kernel Local Configuration Store over the Registry Source
Interface.
.PP
Each hive argument names a hive and an absolute database path. Hive names are
case-insensitive but case-preserving. They may not contain a slash, backslash,
or NUL; the name
.B CurrentUser
is reserved by the kernel. Duplicate folded names are rejected.
.PP
The service creates a missing database parent directory with mode 0700. It
enables write-ahead logging, validates the database schema, creates a new root
key when needed, registers every hive with the kernel, and then enters the
request loop.
.SH READINESS
If
.B NOTIFY_SOCKET
is set, loregd sends
.B READY=1
after every hive has been opened and registered and immediately before it
begins serving requests. Failure to send the notification is logged but does
not terminate the daemon.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/pkm_registry
Kernel RSI device used to register hives and exchange requests.
.TP
.B /var/state/loregd/Machine.hive
Default Machine hive path selected by peinit.
.TP
.B /var/state/loregd/Users.hive
Default Users hive path selected by peinit.
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGTERM
and
.B SIGINT
close the kernel device, stop the request loop, and perform a clean shutdown.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates a clean device close or requested shutdown. A non-zero status
indicates invalid arguments, storage or schema failure, inability to open or
register the kernel device, or an unrecoverable request-loop error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR peinit (8),
.BR peipkg (1)
